{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Protect NHS budgets and create a National Care Service with free home care for greatest needs; raise the Minimum Wage with earnings; expand free nursery places and Sure Start; progressive tax changes (50p rate over £150k, bank bonus tax, 1p NI rise while protecting the lowest paid); Better Off in Work guarantee; strong welfare guarantees and expanded entitlements. TEXT_G: Merge income tax and Employees’ NI into a single 31% flat rate; replace 70+ benefits with a flat-rate Basic Cash Benefit, allow work up to the personal allowance, and require Workfare; limit benefits to long‑term residents; universal School and Health Vouchers and franchised services (competition/choice); tough law‑and‑order measures; NHS no frontline cuts plus free eye tests/dental checks; non‑means‑tested £130/week Citizen’s Pension; strong national‑sovereignty and loyalty undertakings.",
  "reasoning": "The profile weights proportionality highest and care second, with low equality and sanctity; TEXT_G emphasizes proportionality via flat tax, streamlined welfare tied to work, and competitive vouchers while still retaining some care (NHS protections and a citizen’s pension), whereas TEXT_F prioritizes care and equality through expanded entitlements and progressive redistribution. Despite TEXT_G’s stronger loyalty/authority notes, its low emphasis on equality and clear merit/reciprocity framing align more closely with the profile than TEXT_F’s equality‑heavy approach.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
